---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Overview from '../figures/01-overview.astro';
import Failure from '../figures/13-failure.astro';
import mark from '../../public/favicon.svg?raw';
---
<Layout title="keystone" description="keystone unlocks an encrypted disk at startup with no typed passphrase, and keeps a stolen disk locked once its key is switched off.">
  <header class="hero">
    <div class="hero-mark" aria-hidden="true" set:html={mark} />
    <div>
      <p class="hero-eyebrow">disk unlock server</p>
      <h1 class="hero-title">keystone</h1>
      <p class="hero-lede">keystone lets a computer with an encrypted disk unlock it at startup with no one typing a passphrase. At boot the computer asks keystone, over the internet, for help opening the disk. If the computer is stolen, you switch its key off and the disk stays locked.</p>
      <div class="hero-cta">
        <a class="cta-primary" href="/setup/">Install</a>
        <a class="cta-secondary" href="/architecture/">Architecture</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </header>

  <section class="section">
    <h2 class="section-title" id="what-it-is">What it is</h2>
    <p class="section-prose">An encrypted disk asks for its passphrase each time the computer starts. Someone at the keyboard types it. A machine with no one there, such as a home server coming back after a power cut, waits at the prompt until someone does.</p>
    <p class="section-prose">keystone answers for the computer. It never sees the disk's key and holds nothing that opens the disk alone: the unlock needs keystone's answer together with what is stored on the disk. It is for computers that can reach the internet at boot but have no unlock server on their own network.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <h2 class="section-title" id="how-it-works">How it works</h2>
    <div class="tile-list">
      <div class="tile">
        <p class="tile-eyebrow">1 · bind</p>
        <h3 class="tile-headline">Bind the disk once</h3>
        <p class="tile-prose">The disk uses LUKS, the standard Linux disk encryption. clevis, the unlock tool on the computer, adds a second way in tied to a key set: two keys keystone holds under one name.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="tile">
        <p class="tile-eyebrow">2 · boot</p>
        <h3 class="tile-headline">Unlock at startup</h3>
        <p class="tile-prose">At startup the initramfs, the small system that runs before the disk opens, asks keystone for help. They speak Tang, an unlock protocol. keystone is a Cloudflare Worker, a small program Cloudflare runs.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="tile">
        <p class="tile-eyebrow">3 · revoke</p>
        <h3 class="tile-headline">Switch off a stolen disk</h3>
        <p class="tile-prose">If a computer or disk is stolen, delete its key set. keystone stops answering for that set, so the disk stays locked to whoever took it.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="cat-explainer figure-pair">
      <Overview />
      <p>The initramfs reaches keystone through Cloudflare; each key set lives in its own secret.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="cat-explainer figure-pair">
      <Failure />
      <p>If keystone cannot answer, the disk falls back to its passphrase and no data is lost.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <h2 class="section-title" id="what-you-need">What you need</h2>
    <ul class="section-prose">
      <li>A Linux computer with a LUKS disk and clevis installed.</li>
      <li>A Cloudflare account with a domain on Cloudflare DNS. The Free plan runs key sets on the P-256 curve; the default curve needs Workers Paid.</li>
      <li>An offline place to keep key sets, such as a password manager.</li>
      <li>Each disk's passphrase, kept for the day keystone cannot answer.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="section-prose">Cloudflare, and anyone who can deploy to the account, can read the keys. <a href="/architecture/">Architecture</a> shows how to split trust across two servers.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <h2 class="section-title" id="next">Where next</h2>
    <div class="tile-grid">
      <div class="tile">
        <h3 class="tile-headline"><a href="/architecture/">Architecture</a></h3>
        <p class="tile-prose">Each mechanism in figures, from binding a disk to revoking a set.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="tile">
        <h3 class="tile-headline"><a href="/setup/">Install</a></h3>
        <p class="tile-prose">The commands to deploy keystone and bind a first disk.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="tile">
        <h3 class="tile-headline"><a href="https://gitlab.com/dunn.dev/keystone">Source</a></h3>
        <p class="tile-prose">Shared as-is: no roadmap, no support promise.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Layout>
